      "text": "To find the shortest possible length of the remaining side of the triangle, we start by identifying the type of triangle. The triangle has a right angle, so it must be a right triangle. In a right triangle, the square of the length of the hypotenuse (the side opposite the right angle) is equal to the sum of the squares of the lengths of the other two sides. Let's denote the sides of the triangle as follows: \\(a = 6\\) cm, \\(b = 8\\) cm, and \\(c\\) as the hypotenuse. According to the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n\\[\nc^2 = a^2 + b^2\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the given values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):\n\n\\[\nc^2 = 6^2 + 8^2\n\\]\n\nCalculating the squares:\n\n\\[\nc^2 = 36 + 64\n\\]\n\nAdding the values:\n\n\\[\nc^2 = 100\n\\]\n\nTaking the square root of both sides to find \\(c\\):\n\n\\[\nc = \\sqrt{100} = 10\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the length of the hypotenuse \\(c\\) is 10 cm. Since the hypotenuse is the longest side in a right triangle, this is the shortest possible length of the remaining side. Thus, the shortest possible length of the remaining side of the triangle is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{10.00}\n\\]",
